-- title:
--   The nonpivot Jacobian minors generate the unit ideal at the good primes
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime equal to $2$ or $3$, let $K$ be a field of characteristic $p$, and fix a canonical affine chart with pivot $i$. If $A_i$ is its coordinate ring and $J_i⊆A_i$ is the ideal generated by the residue classes of all Jacobian minors $M_{jk}$ with $j,k≠i$ and $j<k$, then $$J_i=A_i=(1).$$ This is the global algebraic Jacobian certificate used to prove that every canonical chart is smooth. Formalization note: the explicit primality witness for $p$ is bookkeeping for the characteristic infrastructure.
-- source:
--   B. Mazur, Modular curves and the Eisenstein ideal, Publ. IHES 47 (1977); Lean formalization of the order-25 case for this mission. Modules in the MazurProof/Keystone/Tate families are adapted from anthropics/fermats-last-theorem (Apache-2.0) via the xiangyazi24/FLT fork.

import Definitions.Def_N25_CanonicalThreeNormalization
import Definitions.Def_N25_CanonicalNormalizedPoints
import Definitions.Def_N25_NormalizedPointPivot

open N25

theorem N25.canonicalChartJacobianIdeal25_eq_top_good
    (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (hp : p = 2 ∨ p = 3)
    (K : Type*) [Field K] [CharP K p] (i : Fin 4) :
    canonicalChartJacobianIdeal25 K i = ⊤ := by sorry
